body:
It's very difficult to polyfill declarative custom elements that allow customization via script, or to implement Vue-like single-file components, without this proposal.

Many of the userland implementation have a roughly similar shape:

```html
<define-element name="my-element">
  <template>...</template>
  <script type="module">
    export default class extends HTMLElement {
      // customizations here...
    }
  </script>
</define-element>
```

but the only way to get to the "export" of the `<script>` tag is to put them into some kind of global map:

```html
<define-element name="my-element" custom-class="abc123">
  <template>...</template>
  <script type="module">
    import {register} from './register.js';
    register('abc123', class extends HTMLElement {
      // customizations here...
    });
  </script>
</define-element>
```

which is obviously cumbersome and error-prone.

The proposal to add an `exports` Promise on the script tag seems pretty simple, obvious, and immediately useful to me. Could it move forward separately from any feature to import from the document?

Totally could see the `exports` property useful! I've also been wondering if the more general idea to aim for is a self-contained JS module system. Hash modules might be a suitable name since they can be reference by their `id` attribute. If we add a new type for script tags it would be possible to polyfill with import maps and blob URLs.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <body>
    <script type="hash-module" id="sum">
      export const sum = (a, b) => a + b;
    </script>

    <!-- All hash modules need to go before this script. -->
    <!-- This cant be a type="module", it must be executed immediately so you can import hash modules in regular modules . -->
     <!-- Ported from https://github.com/tomlarkworthy/lopecode -->
    <script>
      const imports = {};

      document.querySelectorAll('script[type=hash-module]').forEach((module) => {
        imports['#' + module.id] = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([module.text], { type: 'application/javascript' }));
      });

      const importmap = document.createElement('script');
      importmap.type = 'importmap';
      importmap.text = JSON.stringify({ imports }, null, 2);
      document.head.appendChild(importmap);
      importmap.remove();
    </script>

    <script type="module">
      import { sum } from '#sum';

      document.body.innerHTML = sum(1, 2);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```
